<section class="chapter" aria-label="Governance and safety">
  <div class="chapter__inner">
    <h2 class="section-title">Governance &amp; Safety</h2>
    <p class="muted">
      Autonomy is not assumed. It is explicitly tiered so low-risk work can move quickly and high-stakes decisions always remain accountable.
    </p>

    <div class="miniGrid miniGrid--4" aria-label="Autonomy tiers">
      <div class="mini">
        <b>Tier 0 — Monitor</b>
        <span>Measure, detect drift, and surface risks. No action.</span>
      </div>

      <div class="mini">
        <b>Tier 1 — Auto-execute</b>
        <span>Low-risk reminders, routing, hygiene, and updates. Logged and visible.</span>
      </div>

      <div class="mini">
        <b>Tier 2 — Recommend</b>
        <span>Actions that affect outcomes require explicit human approval.</span>
      </div>

      <div class="mini">
        <b>Tier 3 — Escalate</b>
        <span>High-stakes decisions always route to humans. Governance applies.</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="callout">
      The goal is not maximum autonomy. It is safe autonomy: explicit thresholds, visible reasoning, and accountable escalation.
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="chapter" aria-label="Spec layer">
  <div class="chapter__inner">
    <h2 class="section-title">The Spec Layer</h2>
    <p class="muted">
      The system only stays trustworthy if the underlying definitions remain stable. Shared definitions, thresholds, and ownership prevent silent metric drift and inconsistent tool behavior.
    </p>

    <div class="miniGrid miniGrid--4" aria-label="Spec layer scope">
      <div class="mini">
        <b>Definitions</b>
        <span>What a signal or metric means, including grain, filters, and edge cases.</span>
      </div>

      <div class="mini">
        <b>Logic</b>
        <span>How interpretation works so two people do not get two different answers.</span>
      </div>

      <div class="mini">
        <b>Thresholds</b>
        <span>What triggers alerts, actions, escalation, or required human review.</span>
      </div>

      <div class="mini">
        <b>Ownership</b>
        <span>Who can change what, when, and why — with lightweight change control.</span>
      </div>
    </div>

